      trivia: "In 1992, when the newly launched Cigar Aficionado awarded 90 points to the Wavell robusto, some 26,000 cigars were ordered in a single day immediately afterwards, according to the brand's official account. Queues formed outside the Calle Ocho factory before opening, and cigar shops across America kept waiting lists for incoming stock. Even at the 1996 peak there were only 30 rollers producing about 1.2 million cigars a year — nowhere near demand. The founder's son, Ernesto Jr., was a former jazz drummer who moved to New York at 25; talks to perform with Stan Getz progressed far but the engagement fell through, whereupon he gave up music and returned to the family trade."
